2
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Chinatown is a crime, drama, mystery film directed by Roman Polanski.

3
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Private eye Jake Gittes lives off of the murky moral climate of
sunbaked, pre-World War II Southern California.

4
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:54,000
Hired by a beautiful socialite to investigate her husband's extra-marital affair, Gittes is swept into a maelstrom of double
dealings and deadly deceits, uncovering a web of personal and political scandals that come crashing together.

5
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:06,000
J.J. 'Jake' Gittes, played by Jack Nicholson.

6
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:18,000
Evelyn Cross Mulwray, played by Faye Dunaway.

7
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:30,000
Noah Cross, played by John Huston.

8
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:42,000
Lieutenant Lou Escobar, played by Perry Lopez.

9
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:54,000
Russ Yelburton, played by John Hillerman.

10
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
Ida Sessions, played by Diane Ladd.

11
00:57:12,000 --> 00:57:18,000
Chinatown is a 1974 American neo-noir mystery film directed by
Roman Polanski and written by Robert Towne.

12
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:30,000
It stars Jack Nicholson and Faye Dunaway, with supporting performances from John
Huston, John Hillerman, Perry Lopez, Burt Young, and Diane Ladd.

13
01:07:36,000 --> 01:07:42,000
The film's narrative, set in 1930s Los Angeles, is loosely inspired by the California water wars—early
20th-century conflicts over water rights that enabled Los Angeles to access resources from the Owens Valley.

14
01:12:48,000 --> 01:12:54,000
Produced by Robert Evans and distributed by Paramount Pictures, Chinatown was Polanski's final film made in the United
States and is considered a landmark of the film noir genre, blending mystery and psychological drama.

15
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
Released on June 20, 1974, Chinatown received widespread critical acclaim for its direction, screenplay,
cinematography, and performances—particularly those of Nicholson, Dunaway and Huston.

16
01:23:12,000 --> 01:23:18,000
Chinatown led the 47th Academy Awards with 11 nominations, including Best Picture, Best Director (Polanski), Best
Actor (Nicholson), and Best Actress (Dunaway), with Towne winning for Best Original Screenplay.

17
01:28:24,000 --> 01:28:30,000
At the 32nd Golden Globe Awards, the film received a leading 7 nominations, including Best Actress in a Motion Picture – Drama (Dunaway) and Best Supporting Actor –
Motion Picture (Huston), and won a leading 4 awards, including Best Motion Picture – Drama, Best Director and Best Actor – Motion Picture Drama (Nicholson).

18
01:33:36,000 --> 01:33:42,000
It also received a leading 11 nominations at the 28th British Academy Film Awards, including BAFTA Award for Best Film, BAFTA Award for Best Actress in a Leading Role
and BAFTA Award for Best Actor in a Supporting Role, and won a leading 3 awards – Best Direction (Polanski) and Best Actor in a Leading Role (Nicholson).

19
01:38:48,000 --> 01:38:54,000
In 2008, the American Film Institute ranked it #2 on
its list of the top ten mystery films.

20
01:44:00,000 --> 01:44:06,000
In 1991, it was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the Library of Congress for being
"culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant." It is widely regarded as one of the greatest films ever made.

21
01:49:12,000 --> 01:49:18,000
A sequel, The Two Jakes, was released in 1990, with
Nicholson reprising his role and directing.

22
01:54:24,000 --> 01:54:30,000
Towne returned as screenwriter, but the film received mixed reviews
and failed to replicate the success of its predecessor.

23
01:59:36,000 --> 01:59:42,000
The film explores themes of dying and death, sea,
pedophilia, rape, mistake in person.

24
02:04:48,000 --> 02:04:54,000
The tone is intense and mind_bending and dark.
